+  //
+  // The categorisation loop below only ever sees tmp_frontiers_, i.e. clusters
+  // searchFrontiers() re-detected this cycle, and a cluster stops being
+  // re-detected as soon as the map around it stops changing -- which is exactly
+  // what happens when the aircraft is stuck in front of it. So without this
+  // sweep a frontier that is already known when the guard shadows it can never
+  // be retired at all: measured, (-11.60, 9.89) escalated to strike 6 with a
+  // 3.5 m shadow held for 2880 s while the tour went on choosing its frontier
+  // sixty times in the last 300 s of the run.
+  //
+  // Placed before first_new_ftr_ is assigned because this erases from
+  // frontiers_, and that iterator points into the same list.
+  sweepBlockedFrontiers();
+
+  // Both lists empty means this cycle is about to leave the mission with no
+  // frontier at all, which is what the FSM reads as "exploration finished".
+  // Test BOTH: frontiers_ alone is transiently empty early in a mission while
+  // tmp_frontiers_ is full of clusters about to be categorised, and an amnesty
+  // granted there would fire on a perfectly healthy start.
+  if (frontiers_.empty() && tmp_frontiers_.empty())
+    grantFinishAmnesty();
